    50	## 1. SG-INV And The Delta Identity
    51	
    52	**Verdict: CONFIRMED with two mandatory repairs.**
    53	
    54	DG defines, under MI Lemma A, a birational polynomial parametrisation
    55	`t |-> (a(t),b(t))` of `D=A_F` and
    56	
    57	```text
    58	Gamma = { deg_t f(a(t),b(t)) : f in C[u,v], f not in I(D) }.
    59	```
    60	
    61	Target automorphisms are correctly handled at DG:143-145: if `psi in
    62	Aut(C^2)`, then a parametrisation of `psi(D)` is `psi(a,b)`, and
    63	`f |-> f o psi` is an automorphism of `C[u,v]`. Thus the degree set is
    64	unchanged.
    65	
    66	The missing line is reparametrisation. Since `D~ = A^1`, every algebraic
    67	automorphism of the normalization is affine, `t |-> alpha t + beta` with
    68	`alpha != 0`; polynomial degree is invariant under this substitution. A
    69	projective change such as `t |-> 1/t` does not preserve the affine normalization
    70	`A^1` and is not a reparametrisation of the polynomial one-place model. This
    71	must be stated before promotion.
    72	
    73	The gap count is also correct, but its source is not AM-SG. It is the classical
    74	normalization-length formula for affine curves:
    75	
    76	```text
    77	dim_C( normalization coordinate ring / curve coordinate ring )
    78	  = sum_{p in Sing D} delta_p.
    79	```
    80	
    81	Here the affine normalization is `C[t]` and the curve coordinate ring is
    82	`S=C[a,b]`. The conductor makes `C[t]/S` finite. Filtering by degree gives a
    83	basis represented by the monomials `t^m` whose degrees are gaps of `Gamma`;
    84	hence `#(N\Gamma)=dim_C C[t]/S=sum delta_p`.
    85	
    86	Hypotheses: one place at infinity is needed to make the affine normalization
    87	`C[t]` and to make the degree/pole-order semigroup numerical. No unibranch
    88	hypothesis is needed at the affine singularities. Multibranch affine points are
    89	exactly included in `sum delta_p`.
    90	
    91	Classical custody: modern public accounts define the semigroup at infinity for
    92	a curve with one place at infinity as the pole-order semigroup of regular
    93	functions off the infinity point, and recall the Abhyankar-Moh delta-sequence
    94	theorem for its generators; see Suzuki 1999, abstract/citation data, and the
    95	definition/theorem summary in Galindo-Monserrat-Moreno-Avila-Moyano-Fernandez
    96	2024. The gap identity above is the affine normalization quotient, not the
    97	delta-sequence theorem.
    98	
    99	**Repair text.** "The equality `#gaps(Gamma)=delta_aff` is the finite length
   100	`dim_C(C[t]/C[a,b])`, hence the sum of affine local delta invariants. One place
   101	at infinity is needed; affine singularities may be multibranch."
   102	
   103	## 2. Degree-Delta Bounds
   104	
   105	### 2.1 BUDGET=IDENT and DEG-DELTA(a)
   106	
   107	**Verdict: CONFIRMED.**
   108	
   109	For a rational projective plane curve of degree `n`,
   110	
   111	```text
   112	delta_aff + delta_infty = p_a(n) = (n-1)(n-2)/2.
   113	```
   114	
   115	This proves DG:149-156: the Chau-style budget computes the gauge-dependent
   116	`delta_infty` once `n` and `delta_aff` are known. It is not an independent
   117	invariant constraint.
   118	
   119	DEG-DELTA(a), DG:194-199, is immediate from the same genus formula because the
   120	affine singularities are a subset of all projective singularities:
   121	
   122	```text
   123	delta_aff <= p_a(n)
   124	```
   125	
   126	in every target gauge, hence also at `n_min`.
   127	
   128	### 2.2 DEG-DELTA(b), AM-SG, And Tschirnhausen
   129	
   130	**Verdict: CONFIRMED with the escape clause load-bearing.**
   131	
   132	The exact banked AM-SG statement consumed is `AM-CHECK.md:50-63`: for an
   133	irreducible degree-`n` plane curve with one place at infinity, the semigroup at
   134	infinity is generated by a positive delta-sequence
   135	`(delta_0,...,delta_h)`, `delta_0=n`, with
   136	
   137	```text
   138	d_1 = delta_0,              d_{i+1}=gcd(d_i,delta_i),
   139	d_{i+1} < d_i,              d_{h+1}=1,
   140	n_i = d_i/d_{i+1},
   141	delta_{i+1} < n_i delta_i,
   142	n_i delta_i in <delta_0,...,delta_{i-1}>.
   143	```
   144	
   145	This is consistent with the public semigroup-at-infinity summaries: a curve
   146	with one place at infinity determines a numerical semigroup generated by a
   147	delta-sequence, and the first element can be the degree of the curve.
   148	
   149	DG's derivation at 223-233 is algebraically sound. In the notation
   150	`b_0=n`, `d_1=gcd(b_0,b_1)`, if `b_1` does not divide `b_0`, then
   151	`d_1 <= b_1/2`, so
   152	
   153	```text
   154	(n_1-1)b_1 = b_0(b_1/d_1)-b_1 >= 2b_0-b_1.
   155	```
   156	
   157	The conductor formula for a telescopic/free semigroup gives
   158	
   159	```text
   160	2 delta_aff >= (n_1-1)b_1 - b_0 + 1 >= b_0-b_1+1,
   161	```
   162	
   163	which is `n <= 2 delta_aff+b_1-1`. Since the semigroup multiplicity has
   164	`1,...,b_1-1` as gaps, `b_1-1 <= delta_aff`, giving
   165	`2 delta_aff+b_1-1 <= 3 delta_aff`.
   166	
   167	The Tschirnhausen step is narrower than a theorem closing the principal case.
   168	If the smaller coordinate degree is `p=b_1`, the larger coordinate has degree
   169	`n`, and `p|n`, then the triangular target automorphism
   170	
   171	```text
   172	(u,v) |-> (u, v - lambda u^(n/p))
   173	```
   174	
   175	with `lambda` chosen from the leading coefficients strictly lowers the larger
   176	degree. This proves non-minimality in that represented-coordinate situation.
   177	It does not prove the global statement `n_min <= 2 delta_aff+1` for every
   178	principal delta-sequence; DG correctly leaves `OPEN[MIN-EMBED-DEGREE]` at
   179	244-250.
   180	
   181	**Promotion scope.** Promote DEG-DELTA(b) exactly with "`b_1|b_0` or ..." and
   182	with `OPEN[MIN-EMBED-DEGREE]` kept open.
   183	
   184	### 2.3 (R1)-(R4)
   185	
   186	**Verdict: CONFIRMED as necessary conditions only.**
   187	
   188	DG:253-274 correctly records: `n in Gamma`; `(n,Gamma)` satisfies AM-SG;
   189	`delta_aff <= p_a(n)`; and there is a smaller degree `p in Gamma` with
   190	`p<n`, `p` not dividing `n`, and
   191	
   192	```text
   193	delta_aff <= p_a(n) - (n-p)(n-p-1)/2.
   194	```
   195	
   196	The multiplicity computation at infinity is correct after a target linear
   197	change giving coordinate degrees `p<n`: in the infinity chart the one branch
   198	has multiplicity `n-p`. A branch of multiplicity `mu` has
   199	`delta >= mu(mu-1)/2`, yielding (R4). The divisibility exclusion is the
   200	Tschirnhausen reduction above.
   201	
   202	**Repair text.** "These are necessary filters for a minimal gauge; the table
   203	gives a lower bound `n_min >= n_AM(Gamma)`, not a sufficiency theorem."
